{% sec 1, "What Makes a Fund a Hedge Fund?", "What's actually different about a hedge fund?", "What makes a hedge fund" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of two ways to get across town. A public bus is open to everyone, so it runs on strict rules: fixed routes, speed limits, a timetable, seat belts checked. A private car with a hired driver can go anywhere, as fast as the law allows, on any route the driver likes, but only people who can afford it, and are trusted to understand the risks, get in.</p>
    <p>A {% gl "mutual fund" %}mutual fund{% endgl %} is the bus. A {% gl "hedge fund" %}hedge fund{% endgl %} is the private car. Both pool money from many investors and pay a professional to invest it. The difference is the rulebook: because a hedge fund only takes money from wealthy people and institutions, the law leaves it free to do things an ordinary fund can't.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Mutual fund</th><th>Hedge fund</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Who can invest</td><td>Anyone</td><td>Only wealthy individuals and institutions</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Borrowing (leverage)</td><td>Tightly limited</td><td>As much as its lenders will allow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Short selling</td><td>Rare or limited</td><td>A core tool</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Big bets on a few ideas</td><td>Diversification rules stop it</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Taking money out</td><td>Any day</td><td>Monthly or quarterly, with notice, sometimes locked in for years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fees</td><td>A small flat percentage</td><td>A yearly fee <em>plus</em> a share of the profits</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Who sees the holdings</td><td>Published for anyone to read</td><td>Private: told to its own investors and regulators</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The manager's own money</td><td>Optional</td><td>Usually a large part of their wealth is in the fund</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>So where's the "hedge"?</h3>
    <p>The name comes from the first one, started in 1949 by Alfred Winslow Jones. He bought shares he thought were cheap and sold short shares he thought were expensive, so a fall in the whole market would hurt his longs but help his shorts. The shorts were his hedge. Today plenty of hedge funds hardly hedge at all: some bet on currencies, some ride trends in futures, some buy the debt of bankrupt companies. The name stuck to the <em>structure</em>, not the strategy. (The next note tells the story from Jones to today.)</p>
    <h3>Who's allowed in?</h3>
    <p>In the US, a hedge fund can only take money from investors the law treats as able to look after themselves. Smaller funds take <strong>{% gl "accredited investor" %}accredited investors{% endgl %}</strong> (roughly \(\$1\) million of wealth not counting your home, or \(\$200{,}000\) a year of income). Most large funds go further and only take <strong>{% gl "qualified purchaser" %}qualified purchasers{% endgl %}</strong>: people with at least \(\$5\) million of investments, or institutions with \(\$25\) million or more. In the UK, hedge funds can only be marketed to professional investors and certain wealthy or sophisticated individuals, never to the general public. In practice, most hedge fund money comes from pension funds, endowments, sovereign wealth funds, insurers and family offices.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>A hedge fund trades fewer protections for more freedom. Investors give up easy access to their money and public disclosure; in return, the manager can borrow, short and concentrate, and gets paid mainly for making money.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Who Owns What: The GP and the LPs", "Who runs the money, and who owns it?", "The GP and the LPs" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine some friends who each put money in a pot and hand it to one friend who's brilliant at investing. They agree two rules. She decides everything: they can't tell her what to buy. And in return, if things go badly, none of them can lose more than they put in. If any of them started giving orders, they'd share the blame, and the debts.</p>
    <p>That's a <strong>limited partnership</strong>, the legal shape of almost every hedge fund since Jones:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>{% gl "general partner" %}general partner{% endgl %} (GP)</strong> runs the fund. In practice it's a company owned by the founders, alongside a management company that employs the team. It makes every investment decision and earns the fees.</li>
      <li>The <strong>{% gl "limited partner" %}limited partners{% endgl %} (LPs)</strong> are the investors. They provide the money, can lose no more than they put in, and have no say in day-to-day decisions. An LP who started directing the investments could lose that protection.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So how do LPs keep any power? Two ways. They can <strong>leave</strong>, taking their money out on the terms the fund allows (section 4). And big investors negotiate a {% gl "side letter" %}side letter{% endgl %}: a private agreement that changes the standard terms just for them, such as lower fees, more information about the positions, or the right to leave sooner. Many side letters include a "most favoured nation" clause: if another investor later gets a better deal, so do they.</p>
    <h3>Why a partnership?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Freedom.</strong> A private partnership for wealthy investors avoids the US Investment Company Act of 1940, the law that limits mutual funds' borrowing, shorting and concentration.</li>
      <li><strong>Tax.</strong> A partnership pays no tax itself. Gains and losses pass straight through to each partner, who pays tax on their share (in the US, reported on a form called a K-1). There's no double tax, as there would be in a company that pays tax and then pays dividends.</li>
      <li><strong>Flexible fees.</strong> The GP and LPs can agree any fee they like. The GP's share of profits is usually written as an "allocation" of the partnership's income rather than a fee, which can give it better tax treatment. The Fees note covers how it's calculated.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Inside the management company</h3>
    <p>Every fund, big or small, has two sides. The <strong>front office</strong> makes the money: the portfolio manager (often the founder, as chief investment officer), the analysts who research ideas, and the traders who carry them out. The <strong>business side</strong> keeps it safe and running: risk, operations, compliance, technology, legal and investor relations. Life on a Desk introduces each role; this chapter's last note compares how single-manager funds and multi-manager platforms organise them.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Onshore, Offshore and the Master Fund", "Why are so many hedge funds in the Cayman Islands?", "Onshore and offshore" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A fund of any real size has investors with very different tax situations: American families, American universities, a UK pension fund, a family office in Lagos. One partnership can't suit them all. So the fund builds several front doors that all lead into the same room.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <strong>onshore feeder</strong> is usually a Delaware limited partnership, for US investors who pay tax. They get the pass-through treatment from section 2.</li>
      <li>The <strong>offshore feeder</strong> is usually a company in the Cayman Islands (sometimes the British Virgin Islands). It's the door for two groups: investors outside the US, who don't want to file US tax returns, and US investors who don't pay tax, like endowments and pension funds. Those would otherwise risk a tax called {% gl "UBTI" %}UBTI{% endgl %} on income from a partnership that borrows money; going through an offshore company blocks it.</li>
      <li>Both feeders put all their money into one <strong>master fund</strong>, usually in Cayman, which makes every trade and holds every position. This is the {% gl "master-feeder" %}master-feeder structure{% endgl %}.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Why bother with a master? Without it, the manager would run two copies of the same portfolio and have to split every trade fairly between them. With it, there's one portfolio and every investor gets exactly the same return before fees, whichever door they came through. Cayman is popular because it's tax-neutral (no tax on the fund itself, so investors are only taxed at home), has deep benches of fund lawyers, administrators and directors, and has a recognised regulator, the Cayman Islands Monetary Authority (CIMA).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Which door does your money come in by?</h3><p>Pick an investor to see the path its money takes into the fund, and why.</p></div>
